## Summary

Every new branch now automatically gets a standard department template — **Branch Management, Reception, Doctors, Nursing** — so no manual setup is needed. Super Admin department management (add / rename / delete) is fully preserved, and leave-request routing keeps working after department changes.

The auto-creation lives in `db.createBranch` (single source of truth, so it applies to the Branches UI and the data-import "Main Branch" path alike):

```ts
export const DEFAULT_BRANCH_DEPARTMENTS = [
  { name: "Branch Management", nameAr: "إدارة الفرع" },
  { name: "Reception",         nameAr: "الاستقبال" },
  { name: "Doctors",           nameAr: "الأطباء" },
  { name: "Nursing",           nameAr: "التمريض" },
];

export async function createBranch(data) {
  const created = await db.insert(branches).values(data).returning();
  const branchId = created[0]?.id;
  if (branchId) {
    await db.insert(departments).values(
      DEFAULT_BRANCH_DEPARTMENTS.map(d => ({ ...d, branchId })),
    );
  }
  return created; // unchanged return shape — callers still read created[0].id
}
```

**Why routing is unaffected:** defaults are created with `directManagerId = null`. Leave-request routing uses the existing `resolveDirectManagerIdSync` fallback chain (dentist → branch manager → dept manager → top manager), so a department with no preset manager still routes correctly. No schema change.

**Frontend:** `branch.create` now also invalidates `department.list` so the new defaults appear immediately without a manual refresh (`Branches.tsx`).

## Verification

- `npm run build` passes; `tsc --noEmit` shows no new errors (21, identical to `main`).
- Unit tests: 36 pass (added `server/default-departments.test.ts`).
- End-to-end against a local Postgres seeded from prod:
  - creating a branch yields exactly the 4 default departments (with Arabic names);
  - Super Admin can add a 5th, rename it, and delete it;
  - existing manager-resolution / leave-routing tests still pass.


